Collecting events record the activity, collection methods, personnel,
environmental conditions, astronomy, and media for a period of work at a site.
Specimens link to a collecting event rather than directly to a site, so the
event connects each specimen to its site, coordinate, method, and collector.

## Setting up events

Configure event controlled vocabularies and visible environmental fields before
fieldwork in `Settings` > `Events`:

{% steps %}

1. Open `Settings` from the home screen menu, the project `Dashboard` menu, or
   the `Tools` dock.
2. Select `Events`.
3. Customize the controlled vocabularies for `Primary Activities`,
   `Collection Methods`, and `Personnel Roles`.
4. Under `Environmental data fields`, toggle which built-in fields appear on
   event forms (defaults: `Ambient temperature`, `Ambient humidity`,
   `Cloud cover`, `Rainfall`, and `Notes`).

{% /steps %}

**Default controlled vocabularies:**

- **`Primary Activities`**: `Collecting`, `Recording`, and `Observing`. `Other`
  is omitted by default, but you can add it or any protocol-specific activity.
  Previously stored values stay selectable even if removed from the vocabulary.
- **`Collection Methods`**: `Hands`, `Mist net`, `Sweep net`, `Snap trap`,
  `Cage trap`, `Pitfall`, `Malaise trap`, `Light trap`, `Beating sheet`,
  `Aspirator`, `Local snare`, `Pellet gun`, and `Other`.
- **`Personnel Roles`**: `Leader` and `Helper` (add roles such as driver,
  recorder, or observer as needed).

## Managing collecting events

Open an event and use its three-dot menu to manage records:

| Action | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `Create event` | Adds a new event. |
| `Duplicate event` | Copies site, times, activity, notes, ID suffix, collection methods, and personnel; advances `Start Date` and `End Date` by one day and creates a blank environmental-data record. |
| `Sort records` | Chooses the sort field and direction for the event list. |
| `Show QR` | Displays the event as a QR code for another device to scan. |
| `Export event` | Saves the event as JSON, or as a ZIP or TAR.GZ archive when it includes media. |
| `Scan QR` | Creates or replaces an event from an event QR code (Android, iOS, macOS, and web). |
| `Import event` | Creates or replaces an event from an exported JSON file or archive. |
| `Delete record` | Deletes the event with its collection methods, event personnel, environmental data, and media, and detaches associated data. Blocked while any specimen references the event. |
| `Delete all records` | Deletes every event in the project after referencing specimens have been moved or deleted. |

When importing an event, choose whether to create a new event or update an
existing one, then confirm the linked site and personnel.

## Creating a new event

{% steps %}

1. Open the `Events` page from the navigation bar.
2. Select the add icon {% ref id="A" /%} at the top right (or open the
   three-dot menu and choose `Create event`).

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    For consecutive days at the same site, open the three-dot menu and choose
    `Duplicate event`. NAHPU copies the site, times, activity, notes, methods,
    and personnel, shifts the dates forward by one day, and starts a fresh
    environmental-data record.

    {% /aside %}

3. Select `Site ID` {% ref id="C" /%}.
4. Enter `Start Date` and `End Date` {% ref id="D" /%} (future dates cannot be
   chosen). NAHPU generates `Event ID` {% ref id="B" /%} as
   `{Site ID}-{Start Date}`.
5. Optionally tap the edit icon beside `Event ID` to add an `ID suffix` when
   multiple events share the same site and start date (for example, day vs.
   night sessions).
6. Enter `Start Time` and `End Time`.
7. Choose `Primary activity` {% ref id="E" /%} and add any activity `Notes`.

{% /steps %}

## Event details

`Event Identity` stores `Event ID`, `Site ID`, `Start Date`, `End Date`,
`Start Time`, and `End Time`.

When the active catalog format is `Mammalogy`, the `Start Date` picker defaults
to the previous day and time pickers start at `07:00` to match overnight trap
checks. For other catalog formats, date and time pickers default to the current
date and time.

## Activity

The `Activity` card records the broad purpose of the event:

- **`Primary activity`** {% ref id="E" /%}: chosen from the controlled
  vocabulary in `Settings` > `Events` (existing stored values remain selectable
  even if no longer in the vocabulary).
- **`Notes`**: free-text details about protocols, secondary activities, or why
  `Other` was used.

Create separate events whenever different activities need their own collection
methods, personnel, or environmental context.

## Adding collection methods

An event can hold multiple collection-method records so specimens can reference
the exact gear used.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Add collection method` {% ref id="F" /%}.
2. Choose `Method` (from the controlled vocabulary in `Settings` > `Events`)
   and enter `Count` (number of traps, nets, recorders, or other units).
3. Under `Method details`, optionally add `Brand and Model`, `Size`, and
   `Notes`.
4. Save the method.

{% /steps %}

Use the edit icon on any row to update it, or use `Select` to delete methods.
Specimen capture records can pick any of the event's methods as `Method`.

## Adding collecting personnel

The event tab panel has three icon tabs: **people** (`Event Personnel`),
**cloud** (`Environmental Data`), and **storage** (`Associated Data`). Events
also include a media section for photos, video, audio, and files.

{% steps %}

1. Open the people tab in the event panel and select `Add personnel` {% ref id="G" /%}.
2. Choose a person from `Personnel` (project personnel list).
3. Choose a `Role` (from the controlled vocabulary in `Settings` > `Events`).

{% /steps %}

Use `Select` to remove personnel rows from the event. Include everyone who
contributed (collectors, leaders, helpers, drivers, observers, or recorders).
Specimen capture records select their `Collector` from this event personnel
list.

## Environmental data and astronomy {% #environmental-data-and-astronomy %}

NAHPU creates one environmental-data record {% ref id="H" /%} per collecting
event. Toggle visible built-in fields in `Settings` > `Events`; hiding a field
only changes the form and never deletes stored values or removes them from
exports.

Enter astronomy values from field observations or a documented source—NAHPU
does not calculate sunrise, sunset, or moon phase. Record times in the project's
local time zone and leave unmeasured fields blank. Values save as you edit;
check any validation message before leaving the form.

| Group | Built-in fields | Notes & units |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Temperature | `Day Lowest`, `Day Highest`, `Night Lowest`, `Night Highest`, `Ambient temperature` | Measured in `°C`. |
| Humidity | `Average`, `Dew Point`, `Ambient humidity` | Humidity in `%` (0–100); dew point in `°C`. |
| Weather | `Cloud cover (oktas)`, `Rainfall` | Cloud cover `0` (clear) to `8` (overcast), or `9` (obscured); rainfall in `mm`. |
| Aquatic Data | `Water temperature`, `pH`, `Dissolved oxygen`, `Dissolved oxygen saturation`, `Conductivity`, `Flow velocity` | `°C`, `pH` (0–14), `mg/L`, `%` (0–500), `µS/cm`, and `m/s`. |
| Astronomy | `Sunrise`, `Sunset`, `Moon phase` | Local times; 8 standard moon phases from `New Moon` through `Waning Crescent`. |
| Notes & Custom fields | `Notes`, custom fields | Add installation-wide or project-scoped fields with `Add custom field` or `Manage custom fields`. |

Environmental custom fields belong to each event, are not filtered by catalog
format, and are included in event, specimen, document, Darwin Core,
record-exchange, and project-transfer exports. Environmental data is never
copied when duplicating an event.

## How specimen records use events

A specimen's `Capture records` card pulls its context from the collecting event:

- `Site ID` filters available events.
- `Event ID` links the specimen to the event.
- `Method` comes from the event's collection methods.
- `Collector` comes from the event's personnel list.
- `Coordinate ID` comes from coordinates on the event's site.

Changing a specimen's event clears event-dependent fields (`Method`,
`Collector`, and `Coordinate ID`) while keeping the capture date and time.
